kers/differential/14060454/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78889f63dc9099d8f9bedc31a68ad9bd30428fbe2f8467a606dc50e584af769"} {"question" "q-333262" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{64}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060454/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78889f63dc9099d8f9bedc31a68ad9bd30428fbe2f8467a606dc50e584af769"} {"question" "q-333262" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060454/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78889f63dc9099d8f9bedc31a68ad9bd30428fbe2f8467a606dc50e584af769"} {"question" "q-333262" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "22"] ["math" "23"] ["math" "24"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060454/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78889f63dc9099d8f9bedc31a68ad9bd30428fbe2f8467a606dc50e584af769"} {"question" "q-333262" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060454/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78889f63dc9099d8f9bedc31a68ad9bd30428fbe2f8467a606dc50e584af769"} {"question" "q-333687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y-10,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(2)≈-\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(3).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060454/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78889f63dc9099d8f9bedc31a68ad9bd30428fbe2f8467a606dc50e584af769"} {"question" "q-333687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & ={x}_{-1}-{y}_{-1}-10 \\\\  & =1-2-10 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(2,-\\frac{9}{2})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y-10 \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{9}{2})-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2})-(-11))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}=-\\frac{17}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-10 \\\\  & =3-(-\\frac{17}{4})-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{4})+8\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2})-(-11))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-\\frac{123}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{41}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ | $-\\frac{41}{16}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+(-\\frac{41}{16}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{113}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ | $-\\frac{41}{16}$ |\n| $3$ | $-\\frac{113}{16}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈-\\frac{113}{16}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060454/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78889f63dc9099d8f9bedc31a68ad9bd30428fbe2f8467a606dc50e584af769"} {"question" "q-333687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060454/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d631bdad81907be90c2e18f088e459dcc9b4e2e53eba3b3cf77d4bd50716456f"} {"question" "q-333687" "field" "selection" "attribute" 